INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('the_anticipations', '
The Anticipations, written by Anonymous, provides an overview of the relationship between the Tribunal, the Daedra, and the ancient worship practices of the Dunmer. The text explains how the Chimer once worshiped Daedra but abandoned them after the apotheosis of the Tribunal (Almalexia, Vivec, and Sotha Sil), who became the Dunmer’s divine protectors. The Three Good Daedra—Boethiah, Azura, and Mephala—acknowledged the Tribunal''s divinity and were integrated as "Anticipations" of the Tribunal. Meanwhile, the Rebel Daedra—Molag Bal, Malacath, Sheogorath, and Mehrunes Dagon—refused submission and became known as the Four Corners of the House of Troubles, symbolizing chaos and heresy.

Thematically, the book explores devotion, divine hierarchy, and the transformation of religious belief. It delves into the roles of the Three Good Daedra: Boethiah as the Anticipation of Almalexia, teaching enlightenment and victory over enemies; Mephala as the Anticipation of Vivec, guiding secretive and strategic survival; and Azura as the Anticipation of Sotha Sil, embodying magic, prophecy, and cultural identity. This single text illustrates the complex interplay between worship, power, and the evolution of Dunmer theology.', '''abandon'':37B ''almalexia'':45B,130B ''ancient'':21B ''anonym'':7B ''anticip'':2A,4B,71B,128B,140B,152B ''apotheosi'':41B ''azura'':60B,149B ''bal'':79B ''becam'':51B,87B ''belief'':114B ''boethiah'':125B ''book'':104B ''chao'':99B ''chimer'':32B ''complex'':167B ''corner'':92B ''cultur'':160B ''daedra'':18B,35B,124B ''daedra—boethiah'':59B ''daedra—molag'':78B ''dagon—refus'':84B ''delv'':116B ''devot'':106B ''divin'':54B,66B,107B ''dunmer'':26B,176B ''dunmer’'':53B ''embodi'':156B ''enemi'':136B ''enlighten'':132B ''evolut'':174B ''explain'':29B ''explor'':105B ''four'':91B ''good'':58B,123B ''guid'':143B ''heresi'':101B ''hierarchi'':108B ''hous'':95B ''ident'':161B ''illustr'':165B ''integr'':69B ''interplay'':168B ''known'':88B ''magic'':157B ''malacath'':80B ''meanwhil'':75B ''mehrun'':83B ''mephala'':137B ''mephala—acknowledg'':62B ''overview'':10B ''power'':171B ''practic'':23B ''propheci'':158B ''protector'':55B ''provid'':8B ''rebel'':77B ''relationship'':13B ''religi'':113B ''role'':119B ''secret'':144B ''sheogorath'':81B ''sil'':49B,155B ''singl'':163B ''sotha'':48B,154B ''strateg'':146B ''submiss'':85B ''surviv'':147B ''symbol'':98B ''teach'':131B ''text'':28B,164B ''themat'':102B ''theolog'':177B ''three'':57B,122B ''transform'':111B ''tribun'':16B,44B,64B,74B ''troubl'':97B ''victori'':134B ''vivec'':46B,142B ''worship'':22B,34B,170B ''written'':5B', 'scholar', '
The Anticipations, you do not know the author of the book and have limited knowledge of it, explores the shift in Dunmer religious practices from Daedra worship to reverence for the Tribunal. Thematically, it examines divine hierarchy, devotion, and the evolving role of Daedra in the Dunmer belief system, highlighting the Three Good Daedra as protectors of the Tribunal and the Rebel Daedra as symbols of chaos and heresy.', '', '', 'lore');
