INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('steel_warhammer', 'Steel Warhammer is a massive steel weapon which deals average damage. Its visual design is straightforward and functional, with a polished silver-gray finish and minimal ornamentation focused on practicality. To craft it: 4 steel ingots, 1 iron ingot, and 3 leather strips at a blacksmith forge. An Adept smith can craft it.It can be tempered/improved by using steel at a workbench.', '''1'':40B ''3'':44B ''4'':37B ''adept'':52B ''averag'':12B ''blacksmith'':49B ''craft'':35B,55B ''damag'':13B ''deal'':11B ''design'':16B ''finish'':27B ''focus'':31B ''forg'':50B ''function'':20B ''gray'':26B ''ingot'':39B,42B ''iron'':41B ''it.it'':56B ''leather'':45B ''massiv'':7B ''minim'':29B ''ornament'':30B ''polish'':23B ''practic'':33B ''silver'':25B ''silver-gray'':24B ''smith'':53B ''steel'':1A,3B,8B,38B,62B ''straightforward'':18B ''strip'':46B ''tempered/improved'':59B ''use'':61B ''visual'':15B ''warhamm'':2A,4B ''weapon'':9B ''workbench'':65B', 'blacksmith', 'Steel Warhammer is a massive steel weapon which deals average damage. Its visual design is straightforward and functional, with a polished silver-gray finish and minimal ornamentation focused on practicality. You do not know how to craft it, but a blacksmith would.', '', '', 'equipment');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('magelight', 'Magelight is an Alteration spell that creates a hovering orb of light that sticks to a surface, illuminating the surrounding area. It requires an Apprentice skill level to cast.', '''alter'':5B ''apprentic'':26B ''area'':22B ''cast'':30B ''creat'':8B ''hover'':10B ''illumin'':19B ''level'':28B ''light'':13B ''magelight'':1A,2B ''orb'':11B ''requir'':24B ''skill'':27B ''spell'':6B ''stick'':15B ''surfac'':18B ''surround'':21B', 'mage', 'Magelight is a spell that creates a glowing orb of light that can be attached to walls or floors, lighting up the area around it. You do not know much else about this spell.', '', '', 'spells');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('children_of_the_sky', 'Children of the Sky, written by Yvonne Bienne, explores the culture and mystical powers of the Nords, focusing particularly on their connection to the Thu''um, or Shout. The Nords view themselves as the "children of the sky," believing that their land, Skyrim, was formed when the sky exhaled upon it. This powerful, spiritual connection to the sky is reflected in their unique ability to use their breath as a weapon and form of expression, most notably through the Thu''um. The strongest Nords, called "Tongues," can harness this power to perform extraordinary feats, such as knocking down doors with a single shout or striking enemies with forceful roars. The book delves into the significance of speech in their culture, with the greatest warriors being those who can wield their voices to instill fear, command, or even travel vast distances.

The themes of power, identity, and elemental force permeate the text, illustrating the Nords’ deep connection to the elements of Skyrim, particularly wind, which is central to their nature. As the book progresses, it emphasizes the physical and mental toll of mastering the Thu''um, suggesting that the most powerful Nords must suppress their voices to avoid destruction. The narrative also touches on the Nords’ sense of alienation, as they view themselves as eternal outsiders, even when they conquer and rule others. The book reflects the harsh, elemental nature of Skyrim and its people, providing insight into the profound impact that the Thu''um has on their lives and culture.', '''abil'':68B ''alien'':212B ''also'':205B ''avoid'':201B ''believ'':43B ''bienn'':12B ''book'':115B,176B,228B ''breath'':72B ''call'':89B ''central'':170B ''children'':1A,5B,39B ''command'':139B ''connect'':26B,59B,160B ''conquer'':223B ''cultur'':15B,124B,254B ''deep'':159B ''delv'':116B ''destruct'':202B ''distanc'':144B ''door'':103B ''element'':151B,163B,232B ''emphas'':179B ''enemi'':110B ''etern'':218B ''even'':141B,220B ''exhal'':53B ''explor'':13B ''express'':79B ''extraordinari'':97B ''fear'':138B ''feat'':98B ''focus'':22B ''forc'':112B,152B ''form'':49B,77B ''greatest'':127B ''har'':92B ''harsh'':231B ''ident'':149B ''illustr'':156B ''impact'':244B ''insight'':240B ''instil'':137B ''knock'':101B ''land'':46B ''live'':252B ''master'':186B ''mental'':183B ''must'':196B ''mystic'':17B ''narrat'':204B ''natur'':173B,233B ''nord'':21B,34B,88B,195B ''nords’'':158B,209B ''notabl'':81B ''other'':226B ''outsid'':219B ''particular'':23B,166B ''peopl'':238B ''perform'':96B ''permeat'':153B ''physic'':181B ''power'':18B,57B,94B,148B,194B ''profound'':243B ''progress'':177B ''provid'':239B ''reflect'':64B,229B ''roar'':113B ''rule'':225B ''sens'':210B ''shout'':32B,107B ''signific'':119B ''singl'':106B ''sky'':4A,8B,42B,52B,62B ''skyrim'':47B,165B,235B ''speech'':121B ''spiritu'':58B ''strike'':109B ''strongest'':87B ''suggest'':190B ''suppress'':197B ''text'':155B ''theme'':146B ''thu'':29B,84B,188B,247B ''toll'':184B ''tongu'':90B ''touch'':206B ''travel'':142B ''um'':30B,85B,189B,248B ''uniqu'':67B ''upon'':54B ''use'':70B ''vast'':143B ''view'':35B,215B ''voic'':135B,199B ''warrior'':128B ''weapon'':75B ''wield'':133B ''wind'':167B ''written'':9B ''yvonn'':11B', 'scholar', 'Children of the Sky, you do not know the author of the book and have limited knowledge of it, explores the Nords'' connection to the Thu''um, their powerful cultural and spiritual bond with the sky and wind. Themes of power, identity, and the elemental forces of Skyrim are central, with a focus on the toll of mastering the Thu''um and the Nords'' sense of alienation despite their strength.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('chronicles_of_nchuleft', 'Chronicles of Nchuleft, written by an Anonymous Altmer, is a historical account detailing the events surrounding the Dwemer Freehold Colony of Nchuleft, with a particular focus on the death of Lord Ihlendam. The story chronicles the political intrigue and rivalry between Lord Ihlendam and Councilor Bluthanch, who, after a heated disagreement at a Council Meet, were driven to violence. Although peace was briefly restored, Lord Ihlendam later accepted a summons from Bluthanch for a parley, only to be ambushed and killed by foul creatures, possibly summoned by Bluthanch. The text leaves the question of her involvement unanswered, adding an element of mystery to the tragedy.

The themes of political rivalry, betrayal, and the deadly consequences of unchecked ambition are central to the narrative. It also touches on the dangers of pride and the distrust that can arise among even the closest allies. The story reflects the tense and often violent nature of Dwemer politics, where power struggles lead to fatal outcomes. This work is part of a larger collection that provides insight into the history and internal conflicts of the Dwemer Freehold Colony.', '''accept'':71B ''account'':15B ''ad'':101B ''alli'':145B ''also'':128B ''although'':63B ''altmer'':11B ''ambit'':121B ''ambush'':82B ''among'':141B ''anonym'':10B ''aris'':140B ''betray'':114B ''bluthanch'':49B,75B,91B ''briefli'':66B ''central'':123B ''chronicl'':1A,4B,38B ''closest'':144B ''collect'':172B ''coloni'':23B,186B ''conflict'':181B ''consequ'':118B ''council'':57B ''councilor'':48B ''creatur'':87B ''danger'':132B ''dead'':117B ''death'':32B ''detail'':16B ''disagr'':54B ''distrust'':137B ''driven'':60B ''dwemer'':21B,156B,184B ''element'':103B ''even'':142B ''event'':18B ''fatal'':163B ''focus'':29B ''foul'':86B ''freehold'':22B,185B ''heat'':53B ''histor'':14B ''histori'':178B ''ihlendam'':35B,46B,69B ''insight'':175B ''intern'':180B ''intrigu'':41B ''involv'':99B ''kill'':84B ''larger'':171B ''later'':70B ''lead'':161B ''leav'':94B ''lord'':34B,45B,68B ''meet'':58B ''mysteri'':105B ''narrat'':126B ''natur'':154B ''nchuleft'':3A,6B,25B ''often'':152B ''outcom'':164B ''parley'':78B ''part'':168B ''particular'':28B ''peac'':64B ''polit'':40B,112B,157B ''possibl'':88B ''power'':159B ''pride'':134B ''provid'':174B ''question'':96B ''reflect'':148B ''restor'':67B ''rivalri'':43B,113B ''stori'':37B,147B ''struggl'':160B ''summon'':73B,89B ''surround'':19B ''tens'':150B ''text'':93B ''theme'':110B ''touch'':129B ''tragedi'':108B ''unansw'':100B ''uncheck'':120B ''violenc'':62B ''violent'':153B ''work'':166B ''written'':7B', 'scholar', 'Chronicles of Nchuleft, you do not know the author of the book and have limited knowledge of it, focuses on themes of political rivalry, betrayal, and the deadly consequences of unchecked ambition within the Dwemer Freehold Colony. The story highlights the tension and violence inherent in Dwemer politics, where power struggles lead to tragic and fatal outcomes.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('darkest_darkness', 'Darkest Darkness, written by an unknown author, provides a detailed examination of the various Daedra, the powerful and often dangerous entities from Oblivion. The book distinguishes between different types of Daedra, categorizing them as either "Good" or "Bad" based on their relationship with the Tribunal gods of Morrowind—Almalexia, Sotha Sil, and Vivec. The Good Daedra, including Boethiah, Azura, and Mephala, are depicted as less malevolent but still possess terrifying powers, while the Bad Daedra, such as Mehrunes Dagon, Malacath, and Sheogorath, are rebellious and more overtly hostile. The text also delves into the practice of summoning and binding Daedra, explaining the differences between short-term bindings by sorcerers and long-term bindings through pacts and rituals.

The themes of power, control, and the relationship between mortals and the supernatural are explored, emphasizing the dangerous nature of dealing with Daedra and the consequences of summoning or binding them. The book provides insight into how the Tribunal Temple categorizes these beings and interacts with them, offering a glimpse into the complex and often risky spiritual practices in Morrowind. It highlights the varied forms of Daedra, from the grotesque and ferocious to the more alluring but equally deadly. This book is part of a series that explores the broader lore of the Daedra and their influence on Tamriel.', '''allur'':196B ''also'':92B ''author'':9B ''azura'':60B ''bad'':40B,75B ''base'':41B ''be'':163B ''bind'':100B,109B,116B,150B ''boethiah'':59B ''book'':27B,153B,201B ''broader'':210B ''categor'':34B,161B ''complex'':173B ''consequ'':146B ''control'':125B ''daedra'':17B,33B,57B,76B,101B,143B,187B,214B ''dagon'':80B ''danger'':22B,138B ''dark'':2A,4B ''darkest'':1A,3B ''dead'':199B ''deal'':141B ''delv'':93B ''depict'':64B ''detail'':12B ''differ'':30B,104B ''distinguish'':28B ''either'':37B ''emphas'':136B ''entiti'':23B ''equal'':198B ''examin'':13B ''explain'':102B ''explor'':135B,208B ''feroci'':192B ''form'':185B ''glimps'':170B ''god'':48B ''good'':38B,56B ''grotesqu'':190B ''highlight'':182B ''hostil'':89B ''includ'':58B ''influenc'':217B ''insight'':155B ''interact'':165B ''less'':66B ''long'':114B ''long-term'':113B ''lore'':211B ''malacath'':81B ''malevol'':67B ''mehrun'':79B ''mephala'':62B ''morrowind'':180B ''morrowind—almalexia'':50B ''mortal'':130B ''natur'':139B ''oblivion'':25B ''offer'':168B ''often'':21B,175B ''overt'':88B ''pact'':118B ''part'':203B ''possess'':70B ''power'':19B,72B,124B ''practic'':96B,178B ''provid'':10B,154B ''rebelli'':85B ''relationship'':44B,128B ''riski'':176B ''ritual'':120B ''seri'':206B ''sheogorath'':83B ''short'':107B ''short-term'':106B ''sil'':52B ''sorcer'':111B ''sotha'':51B ''spiritu'':177B ''still'':